1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:05,293
Twenty-first of May, 2003. Seville.

2
00:00:05,423 --> 00:00:11,020
The UEFA Cup final. Celtic against FC
Porto, and it has gone to

3
00:00:11,060 --> 00:00:11,881
extra time.

4
00:00:12,590 --> 00:00:15,966
Inside the ground, a wall of green and
white.

5
00:00:16,096 --> 00:00:21,916
Outside it, in the squares and the bars
and the streets,

6
00:00:22,136 --> 00:00:23,342
thousands more who never had a ticket and
came anyway.

7
00:00:24,030 --> 00:00:29,786
How many came? Nobody counted. Every
source gives the same estimate:

8
00:00:30,146 --> 00:00:35,296
around eighty thousand Celtic supporters,
in one city, for one match.

9
00:00:35,990 --> 00:00:39,664
In the ninety-sixth minute, Bobo Baldé is
sent off.

10
00:00:39,794 --> 00:00:44,993
Ten men, in extra time, two goals each,
and the night still not decided.

11
00:00:45,710 --> 00:00:50,273
Hold that moment there. Because to
understand what this night meant to the

12
00:00:50,313 --> 00:00:56,188
people in those stands, we have to go back
to August 2002, to a summer night

13
00:00:56,228 --> 00:01:00,079
in Glasgow, and a defeat that came a
fortnight later.

14
00:01:02,140 --> 00:01:05,733
Martin O'Neill's Celtic wanted the
Champions League.

15
00:01:05,863 --> 00:01:10,012
All that stood in the way was a qualifier
against Basel.

16
00:01:10,710 --> 00:01:13,333
At Celtic Park it went to plan.

17
00:01:13,463 --> 00:01:18,343
Henrik Larsson in the third minute, Chris
Sutton after the break, then Mohamed

18
00:01:18,383 --> 00:01:20,661
Sylla late on. Three-one.

19
00:01:21,350 --> 00:01:26,924
A fortnight later, in Switzerland, Basel
scored twice and Celtic scored none.

20
00:01:27,054 --> 00:01:31,329
Three each on aggregate, and Basel through
on the away goal.

21
00:01:32,030 --> 00:01:35,770
So the Champions League was over before
September.

22
00:01:35,900 --> 00:01:40,874
What was left was the competition nobody
had planned the season around.

23
00:01:41,004 --> 00:01:42,354
The UEFA Cup.

24
00:01:43,030 --> 00:01:47,557
For the people who had queued for those
tickets, it felt like the season had

25
00:01:47,597 --> 00:01:53,210
ended before it began. Nobody knew yet
that this defeat was the first step of

26
00:01:53,250 --> 00:01:57,030
the longest journey the club's supporters
had ever made.

27
00:01:57,750 --> 00:02:02,410
If your club has ever broken your heart,
and you went back the next week anyway,

28
00:02:02,650 --> 00:02:07,832
this channel is for you. Subscribe, switch
on the bell, and come to the next

29
00:02:07,872 --> 00:02:08,698
match with us.

30
00:02:10,740 --> 00:02:16,742
It started gently. Sūduva, from Lithuania,

31
00:02:17,002 --> 00:02:19,025
came to Glasgow in September and went home
beaten eight-one.

32
00:02:19,710 --> 00:02:22,976
Larsson scored three of them in fourteen
minutes.

33
00:02:23,106 --> 00:02:27,671
Petrov, Sutton, Lambert, Hartson and
Valgaeren added the rest.

34
00:02:28,390 --> 00:02:31,787
Two-nil in the return, ten-one on
aggregate.

35
00:02:31,917 --> 00:02:36,183
Then the draw sent them south of the
border, to Blackburn Rovers.

36
00:02:36,870 --> 00:02:42,598
The first leg was tight. One goal,
Larsson's, in the eighty-fifth minute.

37
00:02:43,310 --> 00:02:49,140
In England, a fortnight later, Larsson
scored again in the fifteenth minute and

38
00:02:49,180 --> 00:02:52,555
Sutton finished it. Two-nil away,
three-nil overall.

39
00:02:53,270 --> 00:02:57,983
Then Celta Vigo. One-nil at home, Larsson
again.

40
00:02:58,113 --> 00:03:03,434
In Spain, two goals against, and one for:
John Hartson, in the thirty-seventh

41
00:03:03,474 --> 00:03:04,183
minute.

42
00:03:04,870 --> 00:03:10,534
Two each on aggregate. In August, the away
goal had knocked Celtic out.

43
00:03:10,664 --> 00:03:13,632
In December, the away goal put them
through.

44
00:03:14,350 --> 00:03:18,759
In the away end in Vigo, those last
minutes went on forever.

45
00:03:18,889 --> 00:03:21,626
Every Celta attack was a knife.

46
00:03:21,756 --> 00:03:27,026
When the whistle went, the Celtic supporters,
a long way from home, made a noise

47
00:03:27,066 --> 00:03:28,902
you could hear across the ground.

48
00:03:29,590 --> 00:03:34,688
For the first time in a long while, Celtic
would play European football after

49
00:03:34,728 --> 00:03:38,453
Christmas. The supporters started looking
at maps.

50
00:03:40,500 --> 00:03:46,361
February brought Stuttgart. At Celtic
Park, Lambert, Shaun Maloney and

51
00:03:46,401 --> 00:03:48,294
Stiliyan Petrov. Three-one.

52
00:03:48,990 --> 00:03:54,175
A week later in Germany, Alan Thompson
scored in the ninth minute, Sutton two

53
00:03:54,215 --> 00:03:57,365
minutes after that. Celtic were cruising.

54
00:03:58,070 --> 00:04:03,626
Then Stuttgart scored three. The night
ended three-two to them, and in the away

55
00:04:03,666 --> 00:04:06,876
end every one of those minutes felt like
an hour.

56
00:04:07,007 --> 00:04:10,229
Five-four on aggregate. Through.

57
00:04:10,910 --> 00:04:13,980
The quarter-final draw gave them
Liverpool.

58
00:04:14,110 --> 00:04:19,144
And what followed was ten days that no
Celtic supporter has forgotten.

59
00:04:19,870 --> 00:04:22,478
Thirteenth of March, Celtic Park.

60
00:04:22,608 --> 00:04:25,287
Larsson scored in the second minute.

61
00:04:25,417 --> 00:04:28,696
Liverpool equalised. One each.

62
00:04:29,390 --> 00:04:32,821
Three days later, the League Cup final at
Hampden.

63
00:04:32,951 --> 00:04:38,563
Rangers won it, two-one. The mood went
from hope to grey in an afternoon.

64
00:04:39,270 --> 00:04:44,705
Four days after that, Anfield. Alan
Thompson scored on the stroke of

65
00:04:44,745 --> 00:04:48,887
half-time. Then, in the eighty-second
minute, John Hartson.

66
00:04:49,590 --> 00:04:53,753
Two-nil at Anfield. Three-one on
aggregate.

67
00:04:53,883 --> 00:04:59,540
Celtic were in the semi-finals of a
European competition, and the away end did

68
00:04:59,580 --> 00:05:01,042
not want to go home.

69
00:05:01,710 --> 00:05:07,388
And then, three days later, Inverness
Caledonian Thistle knocked them out of the

70
00:05:07,428 --> 00:05:12,803
Scottish Cup. One-nil. That was football
in March 2003.

71
00:05:12,933 --> 00:05:16,005
Glory on Thursday, misery on Sunday.

72
00:05:18,060 --> 00:05:21,376
The semi-final was Boavista, from Porto.

73
00:05:21,506 --> 00:05:25,617
At Celtic Park, Larsson scored in the
forty-ninth minute.

74
00:05:25,747 --> 00:05:27,444
It finished one each.

75
00:05:28,150 --> 00:05:31,925
So everything came down to the second leg,
in Portugal.

76
00:05:32,055 --> 00:05:35,299
A goalless draw would send Boavista
through.

77
00:05:35,990 --> 00:05:38,434
There was a strange twist in that.

78
00:05:38,564 --> 00:05:41,910
Boavista and FC Porto share a city.

79
00:05:42,040 --> 00:05:47,390
Whoever won in Porto that night would meet
a team from Porto in the final.

80
00:05:48,110 --> 00:05:52,035
Seventy-nine minutes went by. Then
Larsson.

81
00:05:52,165 --> 00:05:56,938
Eleven minutes from the end, he put Celtic
into a European final.

82
00:05:57,630 --> 00:06:03,400
One-nil. Two-one on aggregate. The final
would be in Seville, on the

83
00:06:03,440 --> 00:06:06,534
twenty-first of May, against FC Porto.

84
00:06:08,580 --> 00:06:13,584
What happened next is the part of this
story that belongs to the supporters.

85
00:06:13,714 --> 00:06:17,845
Within days, Glasgow was trying to get to
the south of Spain.

86
00:06:18,550 --> 00:06:21,593
They went by plane, when they could find
one.

87
00:06:21,723 --> 00:06:26,742
By train, by coach, by car, by ferry,
through France, through Portugal, through

88
00:06:26,782 --> 00:06:28,753
whatever route was left.

89
00:06:29,470 --> 00:06:32,864
The ground held around fifty-two thousand.

90
00:06:32,994 --> 00:06:37,978
Most of the people who made the trip had
no ticket, and they knew it before they

91
00:06:38,018 --> 00:06:38,422
left.

92
00:06:39,110 --> 00:06:42,309
By the day of the game, Seville belonged
to them.

93
00:06:42,439 --> 00:06:47,426
The fountains, the plazas, the old streets
by the river, all of it green and

94
00:06:47,466 --> 00:06:49,226
white, and all of it singing.

95
00:06:49,910 --> 00:06:55,528
UEFA called it the largest travelling
support ever assembled for a single game.

96
00:06:55,658 --> 00:06:58,698
Around eighty thousand, the estimate says.

97
00:06:58,828 --> 00:07:02,368
A city taken over, and not a window
broken.

98
00:07:03,070 --> 00:07:06,303
On the other bench sat José Mourinho.

99
00:07:06,433 --> 00:07:11,287
Nobody in Seville that night knew yet what
that name would come to mean.

100
00:07:13,340 --> 00:07:18,497
Twenty-first of May. Estadio Olímpico de
Sevilla.

101
00:07:18,627 --> 00:07:22,093
On the stroke of half-time, Derlei put
Porto ahead.

102
00:07:22,790 --> 00:07:28,046
Just after the restart, Larsson rose to
meet a cross and looped a header over

103
00:07:28,086 --> 00:07:33,162
the goalkeeper. One each. It was his two
hundredth goal for Celtic.

104
00:07:33,870 --> 00:07:38,956
Within five minutes Porto were ahead
again, through Dmitri Alenichev.

105
00:07:39,086 --> 00:07:42,272
And in the fifty-seventh minute, Larsson
again.

106
00:07:42,402 --> 00:07:44,958
Another header. Two each.

107
00:07:45,670 --> 00:07:48,416
Ninety minutes could not separate them.

108
00:07:48,546 --> 00:07:54,162
Extra time. And in the ninety-sixth
minute, Bobo Baldé was shown a second

109
00:07:54,202 --> 00:07:55,196
yellow card.

110
00:07:55,870 --> 00:08:01,655
Ten men held on, and held on. Five minutes
from the end of extra time, Derlei

111
00:08:01,695 --> 00:08:02,774
scored again.

112
00:08:03,510 --> 00:08:07,090
Celtic two, Porto three, after extra time.

113
00:08:07,220 --> 00:08:11,911
The final whistle went in Seville, and
eighty thousand people went quiet

114
00:08:11,951 --> 00:08:12,720
together.

115
00:08:13,390 --> 00:08:17,027
And then something happened that people
still talk about.

116
00:08:17,157 --> 00:08:21,798
They stayed. They sang for their team as
it walked round the pitch.

117
00:08:21,928 --> 00:08:26,738
And the next morning, they started the
long way home, having given Seville

118
00:08:26,778 --> 00:08:28,295
nothing to complain about.

119
00:08:30,340 --> 00:08:33,158
And there was still a league title to play
for.

120
00:08:33,289 --> 00:08:38,870
Four days after Seville, on the last day
of the

121
00:08:38,910 --> 00:08:41,765
season, Celtic and Rangers were level on
points and level on goal difference.

122
00:08:42,470 --> 00:08:46,451
Celtic, with tired legs, won four-nil at
Kilmarnock.

123
00:08:46,581 --> 00:08:50,241
At Ibrox, Rangers beat Dunfermline
six-one.

124
00:08:50,950 --> 00:08:56,251
Ninety-seven points each. Rangers
champions by a single goal of goal

125
00:08:56,291 --> 00:09:00,479
difference. The closest finish the league
had ever seen.

126
00:09:01,190 --> 00:09:04,573
A European final lost in extra time.

127
00:09:04,703 --> 00:09:08,763
A league lost by one goal. Four days
apart.

128
00:09:08,893 --> 00:09:12,427
On paper, Celtic ended that season with
nothing.

129
00:09:14,460 --> 00:09:18,471
But that is not how the people who went to
Seville remember it.

130
00:09:19,190 --> 00:09:24,236
That year, FIFA gave its Fair Play Award
to Celtic's supporters, for the way

131
00:09:24,276 --> 00:09:29,693
they behaved in Seville. UEFA honoured
them too, at Celtic Park the following

132
00:09:29,733 --> 00:09:30,318
season.

133
00:09:30,990 --> 00:09:34,787
Henrik Larsson finished the season with
forty-four goals.

134
00:09:34,917 --> 00:09:39,223
Ten of them came in that UEFA Cup run, two
of them in the final.

135
00:09:39,950 --> 00:09:44,725
Seven ties, from a Lithuanian town to
Anfield to Porto.

136
00:09:44,855 --> 00:09:50,525
The road is still told in pubs in Glasgow,
round by round, as if it happened

137
00:09:50,565 --> 00:09:51,160
last week.

138
00:09:51,870 --> 00:09:55,132
They lost the final. They lost the league.

139
00:09:55,262 --> 00:09:59,223
And they came home with a story they have
told ever since.

140
00:09:59,353 --> 00:10:04,299
Football is ninety minutes. Being a
supporter is a lifetime.

141
00:10:04,990 --> 00:10:10,548
Every match in this film is waiting in the
FanLife archive, with the games, the

142
00:10:10,588 --> 00:10:13,277
trivia and the stories of the whole club.

143
00:10:13,407 --> 00:10:17,511
Come and play it at fanlife.dubelteam.com.

144
00:10:17,641 --> 00:10:22,724
And if this story was yours too,
subscribe, and the next one will find you.
